        <body>
            <div xml:id="d524">

                <pb facs="0596" n="492" sameAs="frus:frus1932v03/pg492"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/4531: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai (Cunningham) to the
                        Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-03-02T17:00:00+08:00">March 2, 1932—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 2—11:45 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>145. Continuing my daily report No. 138, March 1, 6 p.m.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>As stated in my No. 143, March 2, 3 p.m. the Chinese began a withdrawal
                        all along the line at 2 o’clock this morning concealing [<hi
                            rendition="#italic">following?</hi>] offensive on the part of the
                        Japanese which began in the morning of March 1st. This withdrawal extended
                        from the western sector of Miaochangchen to Hanchiatang west of Kiangwan
                        along the upper reaches of the Hongkew Creek. By the afternoon the Japanese
                        had pushed back the Chinese lines approximately one kilometer. Information
                        from Chinese sources is to the effect that the retirement was occasioned by
                        the terrific loss of life suffered within the past few days. It is believed,
                        however, that the landing of the 11th Japanese Division at Liuho forced a
                        strategic retirement. Japanese report that Tazang was occupied early this
                        morning and the Chinese are in retreat toward Chenju. They may even fall
                        back on Nanziang.</item>
                    <pb facs="0597" n="493" xml:id="pg493"/>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>The business situation in short appears to be easier, about 60 percent of
                        the shops including some large stores having resumed business. [Here follows
                        information regarding business conditions.]</item>
                </list>
                <p>Repeated to the Legation and Nanking, copy to Minister.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Cunningham</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
